    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\nMcLaren McL 6GT Makes Global Public Debut in Las Vegas, Brits Announce a McL 820 Supercar for 2027\n7 photos\nPhoto: McLaren Automotive\nMcLaren has been pretty busy lately, which is spectacular for an exotic supercar company, and we now know more about its future plans, including the long-rumored SUV and a new production supercar called the McLaren McL 820.\nThe fall season has been pretty fruitful for the exotic British company. For example, the new McLaren W1 was announced for the Forza Horizon 6 video game back in September. Still, more importantly, that same month revealed a new McLaren Automotive investment worth £500 ($670) million from shareholder L’IMAD to create “an integrated UK operation spanning design, engineering, advanced manufacturing and vehicle development, while enabling McLaren Automotive to build engines in-house in the UK for the first time in its history.”\nMcLaren immediately marked the new business chapter with “the reveal of an evolved identity” featuring a new McLaren wordmark alongside the now classic Speedmark, Papaya, and Speedy Kiwi elements of brand identity. Sadly, October came with bad news at first: the company took the final orders for the McLaren 750S , “bringing the curtain down on a supercar platform that has been one of the most iconic and successful of its generation.” The 750S will bow out of production in 2027, a decade since the 720S predecessor was introduced.\nNo worries, though, as McLaren has plans to compensate for the loss: following its initial private reveal during the 2026 Monterey Car Week in California, the brand-new McLaren McL 6GT made its global public debut in Las Vegas, Nevada. It wasn’t at the legendary SEMA Show , despite all eyes being on it. Instead, the McLaren McL 6GT was introduced in front of the general audience at the 2026 Concours at Wynn Las Vegas (October 29 through November 2).\nBased on “Bruce McLaren’s original, but unfulfilled supercar dream,” the McL 6GT focuses on McLaren's heritage more than anything else with a “lightweight carbon-fiber construction, V8 internal combustion power, and for the first time in a McLaren since the legendary McLaren F1, an authentic manual transmission sending drive to the rear wheels.” The McL 6GT will begin production in 2028 as “a newly positioned and highly exclusive type of McLaren supercar beyond the core supercar line and newly announced SUV.”\nMore importantly, at the same time McLaren has also announced the McL 820 as the next McLaren series supercar, coming next year, and to commemorate the announcement, McLaren brought a kiwi bird statue to central London, completely wrapped in camouflage but also featuring the new name on it. “First used by Bruce McLaren, the kiwi represents a defining part of McLaren’s heritage,” and the company is now using it while looking toward the future. The new model has the McL prefix, inspired by McLaren's motorsport heritage, and also the classic performance-driven numerical identifier.\nThat means we already know the exact power of the McL 820, which is 820 ps, or 809 horsepower. “The naming convention marks the next chapter in McLaren's evolution, creating a clearer and more consistent approach that unifies cars across McLaren Automotive and Racing,” says the company. Quite obviously, this also means the new McLaren McL 820 is the direct successor to the 720S and 750S models.\n“There is something uniquely special about McLaren. Throughout our history, we've never been content to follow established paths. We've always looked ahead, driven by a restless desire to innovate, create and push beyond expectations,” said David Woodhouse, Chief Creative Officer, McLaren Automotive, when they introduced the new brand identity. Let’s see how that philosophy applies to the new generation of supercars.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMcLaren McL 820 McLaren McL 6GT McLaren McL 820 mcl 6gt supercar 2026 Concours at Wynn Las Vegas US\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT New Google Maps Navigation Feature Could Bring 360-Degree Landmark Guidance",
